+      <div class="journal-body" id="journal-body">
+        <p>Every bag of coffee we roast starts with a relationship, not a price sheet. Long before a single green bean arrives at our roastery, our sourcing team spends months traveling to growing regions in Ethiopia, Colombia, Guatemala, Peru, and Sumatra. We walk the farms, meet the families who tend them, and learn about the altitude, soil, rainfall, and processing methods that give each lot its character. Those visits are where trust is built, and trust is what allows us to return year after year to the same producers.</p>
+
+        <p>We buy almost exclusively through direct trade or small importer partnerships that give us full traceability back to the farm or washing station. That means we know who grew the coffee, when it was harvested, and how it was processed. In return, we pay well above the Fairtrade minimum, typically between forty and eighty percent more, and we commit to multi-year purchase agreements so growers can plan ahead, invest in their land, and weather the inevitable bad seasons without losing their livelihood.</p>
+
+        <p>Quality is non-negotiable, and it is evaluated at every step. Pre-shipment samples are sent to our roastery, where our head roaster and two certified Q graders cup each lot blind using the Specialty Coffee Association protocol. We score for fragrance, flavor, acidity, body, balance, and aftertaste, and we only accept coffees that score 85 points or higher. When a lot falls short, we work with the producer to understand why, whether that's drying time, fermentation, or sorting, rather than simply walking away.</p>
+
+        <p>Sustainability shapes our choices just as much as flavor. We prioritize farms that practice shade-grown cultivation, which protects bird habitats and keeps soil healthy, and we favor producers who are reducing water use in their wet mills or experimenting with honey and natural processing. A portion of every sale funds our Origin Fund, which this year helped install solar dryers at a cooperative in Huila, Colombia and supported a women-led farming collective in Sidama, Ethiopia with new seedlings.</p>
+
+        <p>Once green coffee lands in our warehouse, it's stored in climate-controlled conditions and rested before roasting. We roast in small batches of no more than twelve kilograms on a vintage drum roaster, developing a unique profile for each origin to highlight what makes it special: the jasmine and bergamot of a washed Yirgacheffe, the chocolate and red apple of a Guatemalan Huehuetenango, the earthy spice of a wet-hulled Sumatra. Every batch is logged, cupped the next morning, and only released once it meets our standard.</p>
+
+        <p>Finally, freshness matters. We roast to order three days a week and ship or deliver within forty-eight hours, stamping every bag with its roast date and the name of the farm it came from. When you brew a cup from The Daily Grind, you're tasting the work of dozens of hands across thousands of miles, and we think you deserve to know exactly whose they are.</p>
+      </div>
